Task 1: Count replies (strings and files)
Count the lines that start with Subject: and also contain Re: somewhere in the line. Print Replies: N.
Reviews: opening a file, looping over lines, startswith(), the in keyword on strings, counters.

Task 2: Unique domains (lists)
For each From line, take the sender’s address and extract the domain, the part after the @. Collect each domain once in a list, then sort the list and print it.
Reviews: split() twice (on whitespace, then on "@"), not in, append(), sort().
Hint: it’s the romeo.txt pattern, applied to domains instead of words.

Task 3: Messages per weekday (dictionaries)
Count how many From lines were sent on each day of the week (Mon, Tue, and so on). Print the days sorted alphabetically, with their counts, for example Fri 20.
Reviews: a counting dictionary, sorted() on the keys, printing key and value.
Hint: the day is one of the words in the split From line.

Task 4: Top 3 domains (dictionaries and tuples)
Count the messages per sender domain. Then print the 3 domains with the highest counts, highest first, as count domain.
Reviews: a counting dictionary, .items(), tuples, sorting with reverse=True, slicing a list.
Hint: build a list of (count, domain) tuples from the dictionary, so that sorting orders by count first.